Acute Pancreatitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Epigastric pain radiating to back, Profuse vomiting, Serum lipase >3x normal, Pain relief leaning forward

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-001
Reason for Visit: Acute onset severe intractable epigastric pain with repeated emesis preventing oral hydration.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of severe, escalating abdominal pain and inability to tolerate oral intake, necessitating urgent clinical assessment to rule out surgical abdomen, initiate antiemetic therapy, and assess need for immediate hospital transfer prior to the next in-hours period to prevent profound dehydration and clinical deterioration.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The pain hit me out of nowhere and feels like it's boring straight through to my spine. I can't keep a sip of water down."
History of Present Illness: Sudden onset severe epigastric abdominal pain commencing 4 hours prior to assessment. Pain radiates directly through to the back. Patient notes pain relief upon sitting up and leaning forward. Associated with profound nausea and profuse, non-bilious vomiting. Denies recent significant alcohol intake; reports prior history of gallstones.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound, assuming a fetal or leaning-forward position for comfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Dehydration risk secondary to intractable vomiting. Spouse available to monitor but lacks medical training.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 110 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient in visible distress, diaphoretic, and adopting a leaning-forward posture.
Physical Exam: Abdomen exhibits marked epigastric tenderness with voluntary guarding. Abdominal distension noted with diminished bowel sounds indicating early ileus. Scleral icterus absent. Cullen's sign and Grey Turner's sign negative. Epigastric pain radiating to back, profuse vomiting, serum lipase >3x normal (pending), pain relief leaning forward strongly suggested by presentation.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathway to the bathroom; emesis basin at bedside. Room temperature adequately controlled.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Denies regular NSAID or paracetamol use today due to emesis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Pancreatitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Peptic Ulcer Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of rigid, board-like abdomen or rebound tenderness on examination.

Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain characteristics, position relief (leaning forward), and lack of typical chest radiation or diaphoresis out of proportion to pain.

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Negative Murphy's sign and predominant midline/epigastric focus rather than right upper quadrant localization.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe episode of epigastric pain radiating to the back, accompanied by profuse vomiting and tachycardia. Objective findings of focal epigastric tenderness and voluntary guarding, alongside the inability to maintain oral hydration, signify a high-risk presentation. Urgent, after-hours medical evaluation was absolutely indicated to distinguish between a surgical emergency (e.g., perforated viscus) and acute pancreatitis, provide parenteral antiemetics/analgesia, and determine the necessity for acute hospital admission. Deferring care until normal operating hours would have unequivocally resulted in hypovolaemia, electrolyte derangement, and potential pancreatic necrosis without timely intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ondansetron 4mg and IM Ketorolac 30mg. Immediate transfer to Emergency Department recommended for IV fluid resuscitation, imaging, and formal laboratory assays (lipase).
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the urge to vomit, sit upright and focus on slow, deep breaths. This reduces abdominal pressure and decreases the frequency of emesis, allowing the antiemetic medication time to take effect.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review 3-5 days post-hospital discharge.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to ED is required now given the severity of the suspected pathology. If patient declines, call 000 if there is any loss of consciousness, uncontrolled vomiting of blood, or extreme lightheadedness upon standing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a presumptive diagnosis based on clinical signs; definitive diagnosis requires blood tests and imaging at the hospital.
Clinical Course: Symptoms will likely escalate without intravenous fluids and bowel rest. Hospitalization is typically required for several days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately. If you experience worsening shortness of breath, a sudden drop in blood pressure, or altered mental state, call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The injections provided are temporary measures to reduce pain and vomiting during your transfer to the hospital. Your pancreas is acutely inflamed and requires resting and intravenous fluids.
Actionable Steps: Keep a timeline of your symptoms. When pain spikes, assume the leaning-forward position. This mechanically relieves pressure on the inflamed pancreas, providing a degree of comfort while awaiting emergency transport.

